rho falls below 0.5 by ~5500 km and goes NEGATIVE by ~9000 km.
The negative tail is the young Atlantic against the old Pacific. It is
real, it is KEPT, and it is why the global mean is better determined than
a positive-only correlation model would say.


=== PART 2. ESTIMATORS 1-2: KISH AND THE CORRELOGRAM ESS ===
estimator                                           n_eff      sigma
1. Kish, weights only (rho = delta)                  3408       0.79
2. correlogram ESS (empirical rho)                  110.1       4.37
2b. same, rho floored at 0 (no anticorrelation)        6.4      18.15

   THE NEGATIVE TAIL DOES ALL THE WORK: flooring rho at 0 moves n_eff
   110.1 -> 6.4, a factor of 17. Row 2 is therefore NOT a point estimate;
   rows 2 and 2b are a BRACKET, and which end is right is a question about
   the ocean, not about the arithmetic:
     * the deep Atlantic (-108.5) really is young and the deep Pacific
       (-211.5) really is old, and they really are on opposite sides of the
       globe. If that contrast is DETERMINISTIC STRUCTURE WE HAVE SAMPLED,
       the global mean is well determined and row 2 (n_eff ~110) is right.
     * if the basins are better thought of as a handful of exchangeable
       water masses whose values we happen to know, row 2b (n_eff ~6) is.
   The correlated-mean formula is a RANDOM-FIELD formula; applying it to a
   field with large deterministic structure is exactly where it stops being
   well posed. This is the residual ambiguity the measurement does NOT
   remove, and part 6 shows it straddles the decision.
   (detrended residual -- NOT the estimand)      UNUSABLE         --
   detrended double sum went NEGATIVE -- the interpolated empirical rho is
   not positive semi-definite on that field. Estimator dropped, not reported.
   R^2 of the removed large-scale trend: 0.56


=== PART 3. ESTIMATOR 3: SPATIAL BLOCK BOOTSTRAP ===
Model-free. Resample whole spatial blocks with replacement and recompute
the volume-weighted mean. The spread ACROSS block sizes is the honest
uncertainty on n_eff, so all four are reported and none is preferred.

READ THIS AGAINST PART 1: a block bootstrap is only valid when the block
EXCEEDS the decorrelation scale, and part 1 puts rho = 0.5 at ~5500 km and
rho = 0 at ~7000 km. 60 deg of latitude is ~6700 km, 10 deg is ~1100 km. So
the small-block rows BREAK correlation inside their own blocks and are
biased HIGH; the 30-60 deg rows are the methodologically valid ones, and
they are also the noisiest (33 and 16 blocks). Monotone decline with block
size is the expected signature, not a defect.

